1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,612
Here's the same call, in two different games.

2
00:00:03,512 --> 00:00:07,281
In the first one, Quill tells the crew to hold outside the markers.

3
00:00:08,181 --> 00:00:10,415
In the second, she opens the yard,

4
00:00:10,865 --> 00:00:13,513
and there's an answer that wasn't there before.

5
00:00:15,313 --> 00:00:17,334
Nothing in the story changed between them.

6
00:00:18,234 --> 00:00:21,243
What changed is what the Guild thinks of this ship.

7
00:00:22,143 --> 00:00:24,585
Today you'll teach a side to keep score,

8
00:00:25,035 --> 00:00:27,261
and you won't write a line of script to do it.

9
00:00:28,461 --> 00:00:32,178
Before I type anything, I make a copy of my mission folder.

10
00:00:33,078 --> 00:00:37,416
The next lecture starts from the file exactly as Lecture 5 left it,

11
00:00:37,866 --> 00:00:40,902
so today's lines go in a copy.

12
00:00:41,802 --> 00:00:43,179
I call it MyStanding,

13
00:00:43,629 --> 00:00:45,342
I open it in VS Code,

14
00:00:45,792 --> 00:00:47,035
and I run lint on it.

15
00:00:48,835 --> 00:00:50,901
It's clean, and it's the same mission.

16
00:00:51,801 --> 00:00:54,444
From here on, everything happens in the copy.

17
00:00:55,644 --> 00:00:58,486
The game measures a ship on seven pairs of traits.

18
00:00:59,386 --> 00:01:02,838
Honest or liar, generous or selfish,

19
00:01:03,288 --> 00:01:05,440
fearsome or cowardly, and so on.

20
00:01:06,340 --> 00:01:07,959
Neither end is the good one,

21
00:01:08,409 --> 00:01:10,136
because that depends on who's judging.

22
00:01:11,936 --> 00:01:15,002
So I tell the game what the Harbor Guild cares about.

23
00:01:15,902 --> 00:01:18,228
On its record I write Values,

24
00:01:18,678 --> 00:01:22,261
then honest forty, a comma, generous thirty.

25
00:01:23,160 --> 00:01:24,373
Those numbers are weights.

26
00:01:25,273 --> 00:01:27,977
They say honesty counts a bit more than generosity,

27
00:01:28,427 --> 00:01:30,245
and they don't have to add up to anything.

28
00:01:31,445 --> 00:01:34,048
Standing is always somebody's opinion.

29
00:01:34,948 --> 00:01:37,244
So when Quill asks about it,

30
00:01:37,694 --> 00:01:40,466
the game has to know whose opinion she's giving.

31
00:01:41,366 --> 00:01:43,941
I tell it on her record, with one line:

32
00:01:44,391 --> 00:01:46,323
Side, and the Guild's key.

33
00:01:48,123 --> 00:01:50,801
You've typed that line before, on a landmark.

34
00:01:51,701 --> 00:01:55,293
On a person it means, this character speaks for that side.

35
00:01:56,193 --> 00:01:58,430
Leave it off, and she speaks for nobody,

36
00:01:58,880 --> 00:02:01,297
and the number she reads is always zero.

37
00:02:02,497 --> 00:02:04,486
Now the crew needs a way to earn something.

38
00:02:05,386 --> 00:02:07,416
Here's the report call on Comms when we're done,

39
00:02:07,866 --> 00:02:11,700
and there's a second answer: give our fee to the tug crews.

40
00:02:13,500 --> 00:02:15,656
A deed is the word earns,

41
00:02:16,106 --> 00:02:20,432
and then three more: a side, a trait and a number.

42
00:02:22,232 --> 00:02:23,896
The first one goes on the quest.

43
00:02:24,796 --> 00:02:26,329
After the credits I put a comma,

44
00:02:26,779 --> 00:02:29,354
and then earns guild honest thirty.

45
00:02:30,254 --> 00:02:32,974
The crew did the work and came back to say so,

46
00:02:33,424 --> 00:02:35,933
and the Guild calls that honest.

47
00:02:37,733 --> 00:02:39,995
The second deed goes on an answer.

48
00:02:40,895 --> 00:02:44,560
I add a way to report that gives the fee to the tug crews,

49
00:02:45,010 --> 00:02:48,166
and after the semicolon it earns generous thirty.

50
00:02:49,066 --> 00:02:51,181
Both answers still finish the step,

51
00:02:51,631 --> 00:02:54,137
because that's the rule from last time.

52
00:02:55,337 --> 00:02:58,558
So far the Guild is keeping score, and nobody's reading it.

53
00:02:59,458 --> 00:03:01,817
Here's the call we're after, on Comms,

54
00:03:02,267 --> 00:03:04,870
as the ship comes near the Guild's yard.

55
00:03:05,770 --> 00:03:07,661
To a ship that has done nothing,

56
00:03:08,111 --> 00:03:10,386
Quill says hold outside the markers.

57
00:03:12,186 --> 00:03:14,460
So I add a beat that places the call,

58
00:03:14,910 --> 00:03:16,490
and I write the scene for it.

59
00:03:18,290 --> 00:03:19,964
Look at Quill's two lines.

60
00:03:20,864 --> 00:03:24,668
Until today, two lines in a block were two takes,

61
00:03:25,118 --> 00:03:26,835
and the game picked one.

62
00:03:27,735 --> 00:03:32,755
These two each have a guard, in curly brackets, right after the percent sign.

63
00:03:33,655 --> 00:03:36,297
The first can only be said to a ship below thirty.

64
00:03:37,197 --> 00:03:40,485
The second can only be said at thirty or more.

65
00:03:42,285 --> 00:03:45,600
Between them they cover every ship, and that matters.

66
00:03:46,500 --> 00:03:51,082
If no line can be said, the call opens with no words at all.

67
00:03:52,282 --> 00:03:54,196
An answer can have a guard too.

68
00:03:55,096 --> 00:03:57,930
Here's the same call to a ship the Guild likes:

69
00:03:58,380 --> 00:04:01,817
the warm line, and a second answer, request a berth.

70
00:04:03,617 --> 00:04:06,448
After the round brackets I write if and the condition,

71
00:04:06,898 --> 00:04:08,617
and only then the semicolon.

72
00:04:10,417 --> 00:04:13,711
A ship below thirty doesn't see this answer greyed out.

73
00:04:14,611 --> 00:04:16,154
It doesn't see it at all.

74
00:04:17,954 --> 00:04:21,988
Back in Lecture 4, I told you to leave this kind of condition alone,

75
00:04:22,438 --> 00:04:25,103
because there was almost nothing it could read.

76
00:04:26,003 --> 00:04:30,325
Now there is something: standing, with the side the speaker speaks for.

77
00:04:31,525 --> 00:04:33,326
Here's the part that surprises people.

78
00:04:34,226 --> 00:04:36,664
The quest earns honest thirty,

79
00:04:37,114 --> 00:04:39,989
and the ship's standing after it is seventeen.

80
00:04:41,789 --> 00:04:44,127
That's because standing isn't one score.

81
00:04:45,027 --> 00:04:47,830
It's the average of everything the side values,

82
00:04:48,280 --> 00:04:50,930
weighted by those numbers on the Values line.

83
00:04:51,830 --> 00:04:54,455
Honesty alone gets this crew about half way.

84
00:04:55,355 --> 00:04:56,664
Add the generous answer,

85
00:04:57,114 --> 00:05:00,018
and both scores are thirty, so standing is thirty.

86
00:05:01,818 --> 00:05:03,835
And the number belongs to a ship.

87
00:05:04,735 --> 00:05:07,102
With two ships, the quest pays them both,

88
00:05:07,552 --> 00:05:09,969
but the answer pays only the ship that gave it.

89
00:05:10,869 --> 00:05:12,650
So one crew gets the berth,

90
00:05:13,100 --> 00:05:16,030
and the crew beside them is told to hold outside.

91
00:05:17,230 --> 00:05:18,205
Let's play it twice.

92
00:05:19,105 --> 00:05:22,576
First I fly straight to the yard, having done nothing.

93
00:05:23,476 --> 00:05:25,637
There's the call, with the cold line,

94
00:05:26,087 --> 00:05:27,322
and there's my one answer.

95
00:05:29,122 --> 00:05:30,454
Now a new game.

96
00:05:31,354 --> 00:05:33,559
I take the quest, I wait for the beacon,

97
00:05:34,009 --> 00:05:37,294
and this time the report call offers me two answers.

98
00:05:38,194 --> 00:05:39,835
I give the fee away.

99
00:05:40,735 --> 00:05:42,467
Then I fly to the yard.

100
00:05:44,267 --> 00:05:45,639
And there's the other line,

101
00:05:46,089 --> 00:05:47,665
and a second answer under it.

102
00:05:48,865 --> 00:05:51,692
Lint knows your sides and it knows the traits.

103
00:05:52,592 --> 00:05:55,360
If I misspell the side after earns,

104
00:05:55,810 --> 00:05:58,779
it tells me, and it lists the sides I do have.

105
00:06:00,579 --> 00:06:01,906
But now watch what happens

106
00:06:02,356 --> 00:06:04,467
when I take the Side line off Quill,

107
00:06:04,917 --> 00:06:06,708
and lint says clean.

108
00:06:07,608 --> 00:06:10,015
In the game she now speaks for nobody,

109
00:06:10,465 --> 00:06:12,047
the number is always zero,

110
00:06:12,497 --> 00:06:14,285
and the yard never opens.

111
00:06:15,185 --> 00:06:17,542
So that line you check with your own eyes.

112
00:06:18,742 --> 00:06:19,745
Now it's your turn.

113
00:06:20,645 --> 00:06:23,230
Give the crew a selfish way to report,

114
00:06:23,680 --> 00:06:26,391
and work out the standing before you play it.

115
00:06:27,291 --> 00:06:29,080
Then change what the Guild values,

116
00:06:29,530 --> 00:06:32,256
and watch the same answer land differently.

117
00:06:34,056 --> 00:06:37,087
Next time, we go back to your own mission folder,

118
00:06:37,537 --> 00:06:40,617
and look at the tools that show you the whole story at once.
